ives reach 81 or more; all 18 pre-event ones stay at 0). RE-RUN 0.41 (D8b row 9, f09660907): R1 MET on re-run with the CUSUM restarted at each scored window's start ([monitors] roc_restart, default on): AUC 1.0000 on seen seeds 300-305 and on fresh seeds 100300-100305; R2 still at chance; the control (restart off) reproduces 0.7855. The seen-seed outcome was largely known in advance (disclosed). Internal consistency only: no real receiver or recorded interference is checked</td><td><code>iq::campaign (detect, report, runner); iq::monitor::spectral (kurtosis_reference)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Calibration of the open receiver's C/N0 and lock models to IQ campaign results, with stated prediction error</td><td>iq::labfit (campaign, cn0, iqfit, iqreport) and <code>[input] campaign</code> on <code>kshana iq labfit</code>, Python <code>kshana.iq_labfit</code> and the MCP labfit tool (campaign folders inside its work directory only): reads a <code>kshana iq campaign</code> output folder by its DIGEST and groups the scored cells into fit units (signal x loop design x front-end chain x jammer type, never pooled). Per unit, it fits measured C/N0 against stated J/S with the spectral-separation form. Q_eff is not identifiable from C/N0 data without an independent measurement of the jammer and signal powers, so Q is stated, never fitted (the type table, the event's q, or for an unknown type the most damaging stated value, cw Q = 1). The fitted parameter is the identifiable offset delta_eff = delta - 10 log10 Q, with the correlation guard reporting the degeneracy. C/N0 bins locked under 90 % are censored and listed. The threshold J/S is given directly and by the closed form; the tracking-loop and empirical lock models are coupled through the C/N0 model, with drop and re-lock dwells. A seeded cluster bootstrap over recordings (1000 replicates) gives percentile intervals; 90 % prediction intervals come from residual draws; leave-one-recording-out hold-outs are split into interpolation and extrapolation; a pre-registered [validation] table is hashed and each bar judged PASS/FAIL. Report kshana.labfit-iq/1 as JSON, CSV and generated Markdown, with a units block. Synthetic only (no third-party recording, no jammer waveform). The receiver-log labfit path is unchanged</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Synthetic parameter recovery on Kshana's own GPS L1 C/A scenes with a known C/N0 profile imposed from the section 3.1 formula, tracked and scored by kshana iq campaign, against bars pre-registered in docs/design/LABFIT-FROM-IQ.md (section 5.1, amended 2026-10-12 in 4ca3ef3, section 13). The campaigns' C/N0 estimator (M2M4) was fixed in fa388b82, pushed before the first bar evaluation (committed together with the fit code, not alone), after one disclosed diagnostic tracking run. FINDING on the 18-event bar campaign: (c) PASS; (a') NOT MET, delta_eff error -1.097 dB against 0.5 dB (still -0.89 dB on bins at 38 dB-Hz and above); (b') NOT MET, 0 of 50 campaigns cover the truth (error -1.189 +/- 0.093 dB, interval about 0.3 dB); (d) NOT MET, closed form 2.20 dB below the direct J/S at loss, about the fitted drop dwell times the ramp rate (2.09 dB); the original Q clauses of (a) and (b) are NOT MET because Q is not identifiable. FOLLOW-UP (bars pre-registered in 99e7c599, docs/design/evidence/labfit-followup/RESULTS.md): on a stepped-J/S campaign delta_eff is recovered within +0.185 dB (S1 MET: the ramp error is estimator lag); (d) as written on it is met by the tracking-loop model (0.45 dB) but not the empirical one (6.42 dB), so S2 is NOT MET; on a fresh ramp campaign (d) with the fitted drop dwell times each event's ramp rate added is within 1 dB for both models (R1 MET), and (a') and (d) as written miss again (-1.25 dB; 2.32 and 9.47 dB). The method is checked, not any receiver: it cannot be VALIDATED from synthetic data</td><td><code>iq::labfit (campaign, cn0, iqfit, iqreport, schema, mod); iq::cli::labfit</code></td></tr><tr><td>GPS LNAV encoding decoded by an