</td><td>The ground-network overlay: stations with a minimum elevation, the visibility of the vehicle on a stated geometry (the entry state flown two-body with J2, the Moon's position for the cruise, or a stated duty), station handovers that lose a stated time each, and the resulting duty passed to the phase's ground-tracking update rate; the longest interval with no station in view is a budget row</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Zenith and antipode geometry checks and the expected visibility of a distant target in the tests. No real network, antenna or schedule is modelled</td><td><code>mission_thread::earth::ground_network</code></td></tr><tr><td>Relative navigation to docking in a lunar orbit (<code>lunar-orbit-rendezvous</code>)</td><td>The <code>lunar-orbit-rendezvous</code> kind and phase: a joint two-vehicle batch Gauss-Newton estimate over the arc (twenty-one parameters: both states and persistent signal-in-space position biases, common and independent), grown over a quarter, a half and the whole arc with step halving, from an absolute fix per vehicle (AFS pilot code-tracking bound, ILLUSTRATIVE relay set) and a crosslink range, under the crate's lunar force model; the mean normalised estimation error squared, the Clohessy-Wiltshire check on a circular orbit and the 6x6 covariance handed on</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Self-consistency under a pre-registration (xval/mission-thread-lunar/PREREGISTRATION.md, results and the estimator history in RESULTS.md): the mean NEES of the relative state is 5.85 to 5.93 against the band 5.2 to 6.8 in four configurations, MET on the fourth estimator design after three sequential-filter designs missed; the truth and the estimator share one force model, so this says nothing about model error. The propagator it shares is compared with LRO and CAPSTONE in a row below</td><td><code>lunar_rendezvous (LunarRendezvousScenario); lunar_phase; mission_thread::lunar</code></td></tr><tr><td>Landing-dispersion budget at a lunar polar site (<code>l