city checks in the tests. No flown ascent is compared</td><td><code>lunar_ascent (LunarAscentScenario); lunar_phase</code></td></tr><tr><td>The lunar force model propagation against public reconstructed lunar-orbiter and cislunar trajectories</td><td>The propagator the lunar kinds share (GRAIL GL0660B degree and order 150, Earth and Sun third bodies, relativity, DE440 lunar orientation) compared with the public JPL Horizons trajectories of LRO (24 h, 10 minute samples) and CAPSTONE (one 6.5 day revolution, one hour samples), under bars fixed in a committed file before the run</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>JPL Horizons spacecraft ephemerides for LRO and CAPSTONE, against bars pre-registered in xval/mission-thread-lunar/PREREGISTRATION.md (Amendments 1 to 6). LRO: MET, 33 m largest 3-D difference and 2.4 m RMS radial against 500 m and 60 m. CAPSTONE: NOT MET, a 136.8 km end-position difference against 25 km and a 28.4 km periapsis difference against 10 km, the causes not isolated. The row is Modelled because the CAPSTONE bar is missed and the comparison tests the shared propagator, not any filter, signal or budget</td><td><code>lunar_od (LunarForceModel); precise_od; mission_thread_lunar_oracles (example)</code></td></tr><tr><td>The cislunar mean-state propagation against the public Artemis I trajectory (<code>tli-and-cruise</code>)</td><td>The mean-state propagation of the <code>tli-and-cruise</code> phase (Cowell, Earth point mass and J2, the Sun and Moon third bodies, the Moon from a DE440 cut) compared with the public JPL Horizons trajectory of Artemis I over a 72 h coast arc from 2022-11-16 18:00 TDB, at 12 hour epochs, under bars fixed in a committed file before the run</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>JPL Horizons target -1023 (the NASA navigation as-flown post-TLI trajectory), against bars pre-registered in xval/mission-thread/PREREGISTRATION.md (Amendment 1 changes only the request span). NOT MET and asserted as missed: 45.3 km largest position difference against 5 km, and 0.249 m/s velocity difference at 72 h against 0.05 m/s. A diagnostic written after the verdict places the miss in five impulsive velocity steps of 0.09 to 0.22 m/s in the reference (the pre-registered reading that the window held no burn was wrong); over the other 67 hours the model reproduces the next hourly state to a median of 0.38 m. Tests the mean state of one coast arc only: no covariance, filter, update or other phase</td><td><code>mission_thread::earth::tli_cruise; propagator; mission_thread_oracle (example)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Cold-atom interferometer error terms: Raman laser-phase noise, wavefront-curvature bias and fringe-ambiguity resolution</td><td>inertial::cai_error_terms (library functions, not yet reachable from a scenario kind): the Raman-phase weighting function of a square-pulse three-pulse interferometer (Cheinet et al. 2008, Eq. 8) with the white-phase closed form (pi/2)^2 S_phi0/tau_R and a numeric route for any spectrum; the laser frequency noise the retro-reflection delay does not cancel (Le Gouet et al. 2007, Eqs. 9 to 11); the pure-defocus wavefront bias sigma_v^2/R of a thermal cloud and its reduction by a detection radius; the wrong-fringe-index probability erfc(pi/(sqrt(2) s)) for a classical-accelerometer aid and for two interrogation times, and its inverse</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Each closed form against an independent numerical route in the tests (frequency-domain quadrature of the exact transfer function and delay filter, a Monte Carlo over atoms for the wavefront bias, a Monte Carlo over shots for the fringe index) and its limiting cases. One published worked value: Cheinet et al. 2008 (arXiv:physics/0510197, Sec. IV) state that at tau_R = 20 us a white Raman phase noise below -111 dB rad^2/Hz is needed for 1 mrad per shot; the white term alone gives 0.99 mrad (the paper derives the level with its Eq. 17, flicker plus white). Internal consistency and one worked value, not an external validation; higher-order aberrations and 