ovariance from the measurement geometry</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Self-consistency of the estimator and geometry: forward→inverse round trips, the Fisher/CRLB identity J·CRLB=I, GDOP monotonicity, and the estimator attaining its own Cramér–Rao bound under Monte-Carlo noise — internal-consistency checks, NOT an external dataset, so the row stays InternalConsistency. MODELLED passive geolocation — point-source line-of-sight model; no multipath / NLOS, receiver-clock-bias, or atmospheric-refraction terms. 0.30 round 2, against Ho and Chan 1993 and Ho and Xu 2004 (pre-registered 034e1d1b, 1 % of each printed value), a finding (stays MODELLED): the surface-constrained TDOA bound reproduces the 40 N geometric factors (-0.11 %, -0.02 %) and the maximum TDOA; the joint TDOA+FDOA bound reproduces Fig. 6 (+0.06 %, -0.17 %) and, at the source the authors own code names for Fig. 7 (amendment written after that result was seen, disclosed), Fig. 7 (+0.11 %, -0.02 %); it equals the authors published bound code (BSD-style, run under GNU Octave) to 3e-12; the covariance-weighted maximum-likelihood solver attains the printed bound (+0.3 to +0.4 %). The Ottawa factors the paper prints from a Monte Carlo (251, 7.1) differ by +1.3 % and +4.4 %, outside the 1 % bar</td><td><code>geolocation</code></td></tr><tr><td>3-DoF ballistic entry peak deceleration against the published Stardust nominal</td><td>A 3-DoF point-mass entry over a rotating spherical Earth through the US Standard Atmosphere 1976, integrated with RK4 from an inertial entry state, reporting the peak deceleration and its altitude; the planet-relative entry state from the inertial one. Runnable as the <code>entry-heating</code> scenario kind.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>The Stardust nominal of Desai, Mitcheltree and Cheatwood (1999, NASA NTRS 20050042027): maximum deceleration 33.4 g from 12.6 km/s planet-relative with the 45.8 kg launch mass, a 6-DoF POST trajectory with the full aerodynamic database; entry interface from Desai, Lyons, Tooley and Kangas (NTRS 20090004445): radius 6503.14 km, inertial 12.9 km/s at -8.2 deg. Bars pre-registered in xval/entry-stardust/PREREGISTRATION.md before any run on these inputs; both held on the first run. WHAT IS NOT: one published case and one quantity (peak deceleration, which in the ballistic approximation does not depend on the ballistic coefficient); the entry latitude and heading are assumed; heating, heat load and the landing point are not checked against it; point mass with constant C_D, not 6-DoF.</td><td><code>entry_eo_ascent::entry_heating (simulate_entry, inertial_to_relative, EntryHeatingScenario)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Stagnation-point convective entry heating (Sutton-Graves) on a 3-DoF entry</td><td>Stagnation-point convective heat flux q = k sqrt(rho/R_n) V^3 with the Sutton-Graves constants for Earth air and the Mars atmosphere (NASA TR R-376, 1971), its peak and the integrated heat load along a 3-DoF entry, ballistic or lifting at constant bank, on Earth or Mars.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency: the relation and constants as printed in NASA TR R-376, a half-step convergence check, and the expected ordering of heating and deceleration peaks. WHAT IS NOT: no published heating value has been compared (the Stardust papers give a design limit and a convective-plus-radiative estimate of about 1200 W/cm2, which a convective-only relation cannot match); no radiative heating, wall-temperature or catalycity correction, or thermal-protection response.</td><td><code>entry_eo_ascent::entry_heating (stagnation_heat_flux, simulate_entry)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Electro-optical imager radiometry, MTF, relative edge response and GIQE 4 NIIRS</td><td>Nadir imager signal-to-noise budget (Lambertian scene radiance, camera equation, shot, dark, read and quantisation noise), the system MTF at Nyquist as the product of unobscured-aperture diffraction, detector aperture, linear smear and Gaussian jitter, the edge response and relative edge response integrated from that MTF, Q = lambda F / p, and the GIQE version 4 NIIRS with no MTF compensation. Runnable as the <code>eo-radiometry</code> scenario kind.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Closed forms: the diffraction MTF at half cutoff, the one-pixel ramp edge of a pure detector aperture (RER 1), the normal-CDF edge of a Gaussian blur (RER = erf(0.5/(sigma sqrt 2))), shot-noise scaling, and the GIQE 4 coefficients as published (Leachtenauer et al., Applied Optics 36(32), 1997). WHAT IS NOT: no external imager, measured MTF or rated image has been compared; the GIQE is an empirical regression with its own error; no stray light, atmospheric MTF, MTF compensation or aliasing.</td><td><code>entry_eo_ascent::eo_radiometry (Imager, snr_budget, giqe4_niirs, EoRadiometryScenario)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Launch ascent gravity, drag, steering and back-pressure losses</td><td>Planar gravity-turn ascent of a multi-stage vehicle over a non-rotating spherical Earth through the US Standard Atmosphere 1976 (density and pressure), with a pitch kick, thrust reduced by the nozzle back-pressure, stage burnouts at exact step boundaries, and the gravity, drag, steering and back-pressure losses integrated beside the state. Runnable as the <code>ascent-losses</code> scenario kind.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency: the exact identity burnout speed = sum Isp g0 ln(m0/mf) minus the four integrated losses (residual below 1e-6 m/s), Tsiolkovsky's equation on a vertical vacuum burn with gravity the only loss, and the US76 sea-level and tropopause pressures. WHAT IS NOT: no published vehicle's losses have been compared; no Earth rotation, coast, throttling or guidance beyond the kick; constant C_D; not a trajectory optimiser.</td><td><code>entry_eo_ascent::ascent (simulate_ascent, AscentScenario); reentry::us76_pressure</code></td></tr><tr><td>TWSTFT two-way equation, uncertainty budget and link stability with flicker and diurnal non-reciprocity</td><td>The TWSTFT two-way equation 