 <td>BOC(1,1) tracking: DLL scaling and bump-jump ambiguity resolution (opt-in)</td>
 <td>The boc field of iq::track::LoopConfig (opt-in, None by default): the three DLL discriminators rescaled to read the code error in chips with unit slope on the sine BOC(1,1) main peak, and a bump-jump monitor on very-early and very-late correlators at one subcarrier half-period (0.5 chip) that jumps the code NCO towards the main peak when an up/down counter reaches N (8 by default). Without the option every output is unchanged.</td>
 <td>InternalConsistency</td>
-<td>checked against its own closed-form / analytic identity — catches transcription and coefficient errors, but is not an external oracle — Internal consistency against injected truth and closed forms, under bars pre-registered at 700d95df (docs/design/evidence/boc-bump-jump/). A BeiDou B1C data signal (PRN 19, 5 MS/s) generated by iq::scene: with the option off a side-peak start stays false-locked at the predicted ±(0.5 + d/4) chip (measured ±0.549); with it on, one jump towards the main peak at 0.08 s and a late-window RMS of 0.0008 chip at 45 dB-Hz and 0.0048 chip at 35 dB-Hz; no jump from the main peak at 35, 40 and 45 dB-Hz over 4 s; the scaled EMLP slope 0.94 to 0.99 on the sampled signal (unscaled 3.6 to 3.8). The bump-jump detector alone, on correlator triples with the exact BOC(1,1) noise covariance, made no false jump in 100 000 updates at 25 to 45 dB-Hz and detected 1000 of 1000 side-peak starts at 35 dB-Hz. NOT MET (finding F1): the pre-registered unit-test grid for the scaling includes offsets outside the discriminators&#39; linear range, where the scaled readings miss the offset by up to 48 %. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: field IQ, band-limited front ends, composite MBOC/QMBOC replicas, and any external receiver.</td>
+<td>checked against its own closed-form / analytic identity — catches transcription and coefficient errors, but is not an external oracle — Internal consistency against injected truth and closed forms, under bars pre-registered at 700d95df (docs/design/evidence/boc-bump-jump/). A BeiDou B1C data signal (PRN 19, 5 MS/s) generated by iq::scene: with the option off a side-peak start stays false-locked at the predicted ±(0.5 + d/4) chip (measured ±0.549); with it on, one jump towards the main peak at 0.08 s and a late-window RMS of 0.0008 chip at 45 dB-Hz and 0.0048 chip at 35 dB-Hz; no jump from the main peak at 35, 40 and 45 dB-Hz over 4 s; the scaled EMLP slope 0.94 to 0.99 on the sampled signal (unscaled 3.6 to 3.8). The bump-jump detector alone, on correlator triples with the exact BOC(1,1) noise covariance, made no false jump in 100 000 updates at 25 to 45 dB-Hz and detected 1000 of 1000 side-peak starts at 35 dB-Hz. NOT MET (finding F1): the pre-registered unit-test grid for the scaling includes offsets outside the discriminators&#39; linear range, where the scaled readings miss the offset by up to 48 %. RE-RUN 0.41 (D8a row 5, 19c6b3518): MET on re-run with the opt-in exact inverse of each discriminator on the ideal BOC(1,1) peak (the linear scaling stays the default; every existing output unchanged): all 27 grid points within 10 % (worst 4.6e-15, by construction, stated in advance); sampled-signal slopes 0.995 to 1.011 (linear 0.938 to 0.994); B1, B2, B3 and B5 hold on registered and fresh seeds. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: field IQ, band-limited front ends, composite MBOC/QMBOC replicas, and any external receiver.</td>
 <td>iq::track::boc (BocKind, BocTracking, BumpJump, boc11_dll_scale); iq::track::channel (the bump-jump taps and Channel::bump_jumps)</td>
 <td>tests/iq_boc_bump_jump.rs (b1_b2_b3_side_peak_and_false_jumps_on_the_iq_chain, b4_scaling_on_the_sampled_signal, b5_false_jump_rate_and_detection_at_correlator_level); iq::track::boc unit tests (b4_unit_grid_misses_are_the_recorded_ones: the pre-registered B4 unit-test bar kept as written, its recorded misses asserted as finding F1)</td>
 </tr>
