_<wbr>LOG_<wbr>UNITS); <wbr>telecom_<wbr>timing <wbr>(SeriesInput::<wbr>clock_<wbr>log); <wbr>api <wbr>(clock <wbr>kind <wbr>[clock_<wbr>log])</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>clock_log::read_input: one parser layer that turns a RINEX clock file (AS/AR records of one named clock, versions 2 and 3.00 to 3.04), a TimeLab-style ASCII phase or frequency log, or a TAPR TICC timestamp or interval log into a uniform phase grid, with tau0 stated, from a header, or the median spacing, every gap, off-grid reading and duplicate counted, and a refuse, longest-run or linear gap policy; wired opt-in into the telecom-timing series (<code>[series.clock_log]</code>, adding a <code>clock_log</code> block) and the clock kind (<code>[clock_log]</code>, adding a <code>measured</code> block with ADEV, TDEV and MTIE, the table&#x27;s SHA-256 and the phase SHA-256; runs = 1). Without the table both kinds are byte-identical</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Pre-registered in tests/fixtures/clock_log/PREREGISTRATION.md (committed alone, 05bb1d17, before the fixture existed); recorded in RESULTS.md. Three seeded synthetic logs, one per format (a TICC gap of three epochs, a RINEX second clock to ignore), read back by an independent reader written from docs/CLOCK-LOG-FORMATS.md by the same author: grid, gap and fill counts equal and the largest phase difference 0 s (bar 1e-13 s). allantools 2024.6 oadev, tdev and mtie on that phase agree within a relative 2.5e-14 (bar 1e-10) at every averaging factor it evaluates; it does not evaluate MTIE over the whole record, which the engine reports, so that one point per log has no oracle value and the bar as worded is not met there. MODELLED: synthetic logs, and the reader oracle shares the author; real TimeLab, TICC and RINEX clock files from instruments were not compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>clock_log::tests (hand-written logs per format: headers, separators, units, frequency integration, TICC channels and modes, gaps and policies, refusals); tests/clock_log_allantools.rs (the pre-registered comparison…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Clock-log formats linuxptp (ptp4l, phc2sys, ts2phc) and chrony (tracking, statistics) read to a normalised phase series</span><code class="lg-mod">clock_<wbr>log <wbr>(parse_<wbr>linuxptp,<wbr> <wbr>parse_<wbr>chrony,<wbr> <wbr>grid_<wbr>timed_<wbr>offsets)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>clock_log::read_input with the formats ptp4l, phc2sys, ts2phc, chrony-tracking and chrony-statistics: the logged offset of a linuxptp servo line (ns, the line&#x27;s own bracketed time stamp, readings before the servo first reports s2 left out and counted) or of a chrony tracking or statistics log (s, by column position, UTC date and time) becomes a uniform phase grid through the same gap policies as the other formats; a source selector (<code>clock</code>) names one of several interfaces, clocks or addresses; the logs feed the telecom-timing series, the clock kind and the holdover-test kind</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Fixtures written for Kshana from the public line layouts of the daemons, with made-up values (NOTICE.md in the fixture folder); no log from a real installation was read and no oracle compares the parse with another reader, so a layout that differs between daemon versions can still be refused or misread. The estimators fed by the phase series are the ones validated against allantools in the rows above</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>clock_log::tests (ptp4l_reads_the_locked_offsets_in_ns; linuxptp_reads_only_the_named_program; ts2phc_gap_is_refused_or_filled; linuxptp_reads_syslog_prefixed_lines_and_picks_an_interface…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Holdover test scored from an operator-supplied time-error record</span><code class="lg-mod">holdover_<wbr>test <wbr>(HoldoverTestScenario,<wbr> <wbr>recovery,<wbr> <wbr>linear_<wbr>fit); <wbr>telecom_<wbr>timing <wbr>(check_<wbr>mask,<wbr> <wbr>check_<wbr>envelope,<wbr> <wbr>budget_<wbr>results)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>holdover-test</code> kind: a time-error record (a linuxptp, chrony, TICC, TimeLab or RINEX clock log, or time_s,time_error_ns pairs) is split at the two times the operator states for GNSS removal and restoration into a baseline, a holdover and a recovery step; the baseline is measured against the ITU-T masks written for a locked clock and the holdover against those written for holdover or an autonomous clock, the ePRTC-A holdover envelope and the time-error budgets; the holdover reports the least-squares frequency offset the system held, and the recovery whether and when |TE| came inside a stated band and stayed for a stated dwell, with the peak and the largest step. Every limit is the one the telecom-timing kind transcribes; the two times are the operator&#x27;s statement and are not verified</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Hand computation on records built from formulas whose slope, step, extremum and recovery time are known before the run (the tests state them), and the masks, envelope and budgets of the telecom-timing rows above. No laboratory measurement of a real holdover was scored and no external oracle was compared, so the kind makes no claim about any clock or system; a timing-lab partner is needed before any per-clock-class figure could be promoted</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>holdover_test::tests (the_record_is_split_at_the_stated_times; holdover_figures_match_a_hand_computation; recovery_is_timed_from_the_restoration; masks_are_measured_on_the_step_their_condition_names…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">GNSS signal availability in the Space Service Volume and beyond, compared with the ICG SSV booklet</span><code class="lg-mod">ssv <wbr>(constellations,<wbr> <wbr>geom,<wbr> <wbr>sweep,<wbr> <wbr>pattern,<wbr> <wbr>check)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>gnss-ssv</code> kind&#x27;s availability sweeps: per constellation (GPS, GLONASS, Galileo, BDS, QZSS, NavIC) and combined, the share of (user, epoch) pairs with at least one and at least four signals and the longest outage, for a user on a sphere (the booklet&#x27;s 2,562-point global shell, 14 days at 1 minute) or on a trajectory (GEO, HEO, lunar transfer), from the Keplerian orbits of the booklet&#x27;s annex C, a constant minimum radiated power inside the reference off-boresight angle, Earth blockage by the sphere plus 50 km and a C/N0 threshold; user antennas from the booklet&#x27;s table B10</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The ICG Space Service Volume booklet (UN OOSA ST/SPACE/75, 2018), itself a simulation, compared under a pre-registration committed before the run (xval/gnss-ssv/PREREGISTRATION.md; results in xval/gnss-ssv/RESULTS.md, pinned by tests/ssv_booklet_results.rs, misses included). Run 1 on the pre-registered reading: the minimum-radiated-power constants and the lunar bar agree (S0, S6 met); the upper and lower SSV, RF-threshold, GEO and HEO bars are NOT MET, almost all through GLONASS, for which the booklet gives 20 and 28 degrees in tables B2 and B6 and 26 and 34 degrees in table 4.2. A diagnostic variant with table 4.2&#x27;s angles agrees in every cell of the upper SSV, lower SSV and the six GEO missions (it does not replace run 1; RF, HEO and lunar cells still miss). The measured GPS L1 panel patterns are not reproduced by the parametric stand-in (RMS 6 to 9 dB against a 3 dB bar). Agreement shows the booklet&#x27;s stated method is reproduced, not that GNSS signals behave so</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>ssv::geom::tests (the_grid_has_the_booklets_2562_points_on_the_unit_sphere; the_interval_form_equals_the_general_predicate; a_user_behind_the_earth_is_blocked_and_one_beside_the_limb_is_not); ssv::sweep::tests…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Limb-ray ionosphere, plasmasphere and atmosphere effects on grazing GNSS signals, and the weak-signal receiver design, in the <code>gnss-ssv</code> kind</span><code class="lg-mod">ssv <wbr>(limb,<wbr> <wbr>weak)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>A Chapman ionosphere, a Gallagher-type plasmasphere and a neutral atmosphere (refractivity bending, oxygen absorption, defocusing) integrated along the straight ray between transmitter and user: slant total electron content, its plasmaspheric part, excess delay, tangent altitude and the loss on grazing links; and a weak-signal receiver design (coherent and non-coherent integration, data-bit limit, aiding, Doppler search) that sets the C/N0 threshold from the acquisition and tracking-loop models</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Closed forms and published magnitudes in the tests (a Chapman layer&#x27;s peak and the bending of the surface limb, a few TECU of plasmasphere on a GPS-to-GEO ray); the receiver design reuses the acquisition and tracking-loop models and their tests. No measured limb-sounding or weak-signal reception data is compared with it</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>ssv::limb::tests (the_chapman_layer_peaks_at_its_height_with_its_density; the_surface_limb_bends_about_one_and_a_third_degrees; plasmaspheric_tec_is_a_few_tecu_for_an_equatorial_gps_to_geo_ray); ssv::weak::tests…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Onboard GNSS navigation filter for high-orbit and lunar-transfer spacecraft</span><code class="lg-mod">ssv::<wbr>nav <wbr>(run_<wbr>nav,<wbr> <wbr>Ekf)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>gnss-ssv</code> kind&#x27;s <code>[navigation]</code> mode: a nine-state extended Kalman filter (position, velocity, receiver clock bias and drift, a solar-radiation-pressure scale) on pseudoranges whose availability, noise and C/N0 come from the same link geometry as the sweeps; truth is integrated with J2, Sun and Moon third-body and radiation pressure with shadow, the filter models J2 and the third bodies and estimates the radiation pressure; reports position and velocity errors inside and above the GNSS orbits, the longest measurement gap and the mean NEES</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Simulated truth against the filter&#x27;s own estimate (a consistent filter&#x27;s mean normalised estimation error squared is about 3 for the position) and determinism for a fixed seed. No flight or recorded high-orbit GNSS data is compared; the Sun and Moon are low-precision circular stand-ins</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>ssv::nav::tests (the_filter_converges_where_signals_are_plentiful_and_is_deterministic; the_covariance_is_consistent_with_the_error; without_signals_the_filter_coasts_and_the_gap_is_reported); ssv::scenario::tests…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Relative navigation of a multi-spacecraft formation in a highly elliptical orbit from GNSS</span><code class="lg-mod">ssv::<wbr>formation <wbr>(run_<wbr>formation,<wbr> <wbr>RelEkf)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>gnss-ssv</code> kind&#x27;s <code>[formation]</code> mode: a chief and deputies each run the absolute filter; the deputy&#x27;s position relative to the chief is estimated by subtracting the absolute estimates and by a relative filter on single-differenced pseudoranges of the signals both craft track, with the relative dynamics the difference of two nonlinear trajectories</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The same simulated truth as the absolute filter: the relative filter&#x27;s error against the true relative state, and the check that it does at least as well as subtracting two independent absolute fixes. No flight formation data is compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>ssv::formation::tests (differencing_beats_subtracting_absolute_estimates_and_is_deterministic); ssv::scenario::tests (navigation_and_formation_run_and_report; formation_needs_navigation_and_one_mode_only)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Navigation and timing error budget of a chain of mission phases from launch to entry (<code>mission-thread</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">mission_<wbr>thread <wbr>(scenario,<wbr> <wbr>handover,<wbr> <wbr>conversions,<wbr> <wbr>budget,<wbr> <wbr>phase)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>mission-thread</code> kind: a document of phases, each one of eight registered kinds, run in order with the 6x6 covariance, the clock and the frame handed on under a stated rule (<code>carry</code>; <code>inflate</code>, white acceleration noise over a stated gap on a straight-line model; <code>reacquire</code>, dead reckoning then the source floor, never below it, never a reset); frame conversions GCRF, MCI and MCMF and timescale conversions through TT, each its own budget row, with the Moon&#x27;s state from an optional DE440 kernel cut and the analytic series as the stated fallback; the margin against a stated requirement, the weakest link, the events and a timeline figure. Every number is a stated input or a model of one</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Hand calculations in the tests (the straight-line covariance growth, the mean sidereal rotation, the secular lunar clock rate) and, for the Moon&#x27;s state only, the committed DE440 kernel cuts against pinned JPL Horizons values (worst 2.6 m and 7e-6 m/s). No mission data is compared with the chain; its phases are rows of their own. The document is a stated chain with ILLUSTRATIVE inputs, public sources only and no agency endorsement</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>mission_thread::scenario::tests::a_thread_runs_hands_over_converts_and_names_the_weakest_link; mission_thread::scenario::tests::bad_documents_fail_with_the_reason…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Launch and ascent to a parking orbit with GNSS acquisition and fade (<code>launch-ascent</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">mission_<wbr>thread::<wbr>earth::<wbr>launch <wbr>(LaunchAscent)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>launch-ascent</code> phase: a constant-velocity position and velocity covariance with white acceleration noise (the dead-reckoning grade), a GNSS fix of stated accuracy applied after the acquisition time and outside stated fades, a bias-and-drift clock disciplined by the same fixes, and the parking-orbit insertion state</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The Riccati steady state and the hand calculation of the prediction in the tests. Nothing here models a launch vehicle or a receiver; every number is a stated input</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>mission_thread::earth::launch::tests::with_gnss_the_sigma_settles_near_the_fix_and_an_outage_lets_it_grow; mission_thread::earth::launch::tests::before_acquisition_the_sigma_only_grows_and_a_longer_acquisition_is_worse…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Relative navigation to the docking envelope in low Earth orbit over sensor legs (<code>leo-rendezvous</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">mission_<wbr>thread::<wbr>earth::<wbr>rendezvous <wbr>(LeoRendezvous); <wbr>cw_<wbr>dynamics; <wbr>mission_<wbr>thread::<wbr>sensors</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>leo-rendezvous</code> phase: the relative state in the target&#x27;s LVLH axes with the closed-form Clohessy-Wiltshire transition, a 6x6 Kalman covariance with white acceleration noise and position updates whose variance is the sensor&#x27;s at the current range, over legs of a stated sensor (ILLUSTRATIVE optical camera, lidar and radio-ranging forms); the docking window is the time from which the relative sigma stays inside the capture envelope to the end of the phase. The chaser&#x27;s absolute covariance is handed on unchanged and a row says so</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>A one-axis hand calculation of the predict and update in the test; the Clohessy-Wiltshire transition is the one cw_dynamics checks against an independent numerical integration. The sensors are ILLUSTRATIVE and no real rendezvous is compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>mission_thread::earth::rendezvous::tests::the_relative_sigma_falls_through_the_legs_and_opens_a_docking_window; mission_thread::earth::rendezvous::tests::without_relative_sensing_the_sigma_only_grows_and_no_window_opens…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Trans-lunar injection and cislunar cruise: propagated mean state and covariance with tracking updates (<code>tli-and-cruise</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">mission_<wbr>thread::<wbr>earth::<wbr>tli_<wbr>cruise <wbr>(TliCruise); <wbr>propagator; <wbr>ssv::<wbr>sweep</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>tli-and-cruise</code> phase: the mean state by the engine&#x27;s Cowell propagator (Earth point mass and J2, the Sun and Moon as third bodies, the Moon from the thread&#x27;s DE440 kernel where it covers the segment and the Sun from the analytic series); the covariance by a forward-difference state-transition matrix of that propagation (the nominal run and six perturbed runs per segment) with white acceleration noise; the TLI burn&#x27;s size and pointing error; <code>gnss-ssv</code> fixes whose availability and PDOP come from the space-service-volume sweep along an osculating orbit standing in for the arc; range and range-rate tracking along the geocentric direction scaled by the ground-network duty, with the Joseph-form update</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Liouville&#x27;s theorem (the determinant of the state-transition matrix of the conservative part is one) and a finer difference in the tests; the mean state is compared with a public trajectory in the row below, which it misses. The burn error, the updates and the covariance are not compared with anything</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>mission_thread::earth::tli_cruise::tests::the_stm_is_symplectic_for_the_conservative_part_and_matches_a_finer_difference; mission_thread::earth::tli_cruise::tests::with_no_tracking_the_covariance_is_the_stm_propagation_…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Entry interface covariance, corridor probability, peak deceleration and landing footprint (<code>entry</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">mission_<wbr>thread::<wbr>earth::<wbr>entry <wbr>(Entry); <wbr>reentry</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>entry</code> phase: the return coast of <code>tli-and-cruise</code> with no burn, ending at the entry interface; the flight-path angle and its sigma from the full covariance by a finite-difference gradient; the probability that the angle is inside a stated corridor; the peak deceleration at the nominal angle and at both corridor edges from the engine&#x27;s planar ballistic point-mass entry (skip-out detected) beside the Allen-Eggers closed form; the landing footprint from a stated downrange sensitivity and the interface covariance across the orbit plane. No lift, guidance or heating is modelled</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Hand calculations in the tests (the angle sigma is the perpendicular velocity sigma over the speed; the Gaussian mass in a corridor). The planar entry is the engine&#x27;s own and the Allen-Eggers form is a closed-form comparison; no flown entry is compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>mission_thread::earth::entry::tests::the_flight_path_angle_and_its_sigma_follow_the_velocity_error_over_the_speed; mission_thread::earth::entry::tests::the_corridor_probability_is_the_gaussian_mass_inside…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Ground-network coverage and tracking duty for a thread phase (<code>[ground_network]</code> of <code>mission-thread</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">mission_<wbr>thread::<wbr>earth::<wbr>ground_<wbr>network</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The ground-network overlay: stations with a minimum elevation, the visibility of the vehicle on a stated geometry (the entry state flown two-body with J2, the Moon&#x27;s position for the cruise, or a stated duty), station handovers that lose a stated time each, and the resulting duty passed to the phase&#x27;s ground-tracking update rate; the longest interval with no station in view is a budget row</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Zenith and antipode geometry checks and the expected visibility of a distant target in the tests. No real network, antenna or schedule is modelled</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>mission_thread::earth::ground_network::tests::a_vehicle_over_a_station_is_seen_and_one_on_the_other_side_of_the_earth_is_not; mission_thread::earth::ground_network::tests::the_moon_is_in_view_of_three_well_spread_statio…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Relative navigation to docking in a lunar orbit (<code>lunar-orbit-rendezvous</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">lunar_<wbr>rendezvous <wbr>(LunarRendezvousScenario); <wbr>lunar_<wbr>phase; <wbr>mission_<wbr>thread::<wbr>lunar</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>lunar-orbit-rendezvous</code> kind and phase: a joint two-vehicle batch Gauss-Newton estimate over the arc (twenty-one parameters: both states and persistent signal-in-space position biases, common and independent), grown over a quarter, a half and the whole arc with step halving, from an absolute fix per vehicle (AFS pilot code-tracking bound, ILLUSTRATIVE relay set) and a crosslink range, under the crate&#x27;s lunar force model; the mean normalised estimation error squared, the Clohessy-Wiltshire check on a circular orbit and the 6x6 covariance handed on</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Self-consistency under a pre-registration (xval/mission-thread-lunar/PREREGISTRATION.md, results and the estimator history in RESULTS.md): the mean NEES of the relative state is 5.85 to 5.93 against the band 5.2 to 6.8 in four configurations, MET on the fourth estimator design after three sequential-filter designs missed; the truth and the estimator share one force model, so this says nothing about model error. The propagator it shares is compared with LRO and CAPSTONE in a row below</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>lunar_rendezvous::tests::b1_mean_nees_is_inside_the_registered_band_in_all_four_configurations; lunar_rendezvous::tests::circular_cw_check_is_within_the_pre_registered_bar…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Landing-dispersion budget at a lunar polar site (<code>lunar-descent</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">lunar_<wbr>landing <wbr>(LunarLandingScenario); <wbr>lunar_<wbr>phase; <wbr>terrain_<wbr>nav</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>lunar-descent</code> kind and phase: a five-state per-axis inertial error model (position, velocity, accelerometer bias, tilt, gyro bias) over a powered descent, aided by AFS fixes (standalone or differential, with the clock-aided three-relay case), a terrain-relative fix (the engine&#x27;s terrain-nav matcher on synthetic relief, or a Cramer-Rao bound on a LOLA DEM with an aperture-problem rule) and lunar time, with a Monte Carlo touchdown ellipse</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The closed-form inertial drift, the reused kinds&#x27; own results and the calibration of the 3-sigma ellipse (the fraction inside agrees with its expectation) in the tests. No flown or tested landing is compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>lunar_landing::tests::b4_with_no_aid_the_landing_error_is_the_closed_form_inertial_drift; lunar_landing::tests::b4_each_aid_reports_exactly_what_the_reused_kind_gives; lunar_landing::tests::b3_ellipse_calibration…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Surface positioning and timing availability by site and season at the lunar poles (<code>lunar-surface</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">lunar_<wbr>surface_<wbr>map <wbr>(LunarSurfaceMapScenario); <wbr>lunar_<wbr>phase</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>lunar-surface</code> kind and phase: relay availability and PDOP, Earth and Sun visibility and a disciplined surface clock for each site and season, with the terrain skyline of <code>moonlight-service-volume</code>; a single-site run equals that kind&#x27;s availability and PDOP to the bit</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Exact equality with the existing kind for one site, and monotonicity checks, in the tests. The relay set is ILLUSTRATIVE and no surface measurement is compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>lunar_surface_map::tests::b5_equals_the_service_volume_for_a_single_site; lunar_surface_map::tests::b5_equals_the_service_volume_with_the_real_terrain; lunar_surface_map::tests::terrain_never_adds_availability…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Powered ascent from a lunar polar site to an insertion orbit (<code>lunar-ascent</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">lunar_<wbr>ascent <wbr>(LunarAscentScenario); <wbr>lunar_<wbr>phase</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>lunar-ascent</code> kind and phase: the five-state per-axis inertial error model run over a powered ascent with specific force along the path, aided by AFS fixes and lunar time, reporting the insertion position and velocity covariance carried to orbit and the engine-cutoff dispersion (reported, not carried)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The closed-form inertial drift and monotonicity checks in the tests. No flown ascent is compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>lunar_ascent::tests::with_no_aid_the_insertion_error_is_the_closed_form_inertial_drift; lunar_ascent::tests::the_aid_never_hurts_and_the_velocity_is_carried_with_its_correlation…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">The lunar force model propagation against public reconstructed lunar-orbiter and cislunar trajectories</span><code class="lg-mod">lunar_<wbr>od <wbr>(LunarForceModel); <wbr>precise_<wbr>od; <wbr>mission_<wbr>thread_<wbr>lunar_<wbr>oracles <wbr>(example)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The propagator the lunar kinds share (GRAIL GL0660B degree and order 150, Earth and Sun third bodies, relativity, DE440 lunar orientation) compared with the public JPL Horizons trajectories of LRO (24 h, 10 minute samples) and CAPSTONE (one 6.5 day revolution, one hour samples), under bars fixed in a committed file before the run</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>JPL Horizons spacecraft ephemerides for LRO and CAPSTONE, against bars pre-registered in xval/mission-thread-lunar/PREREGISTRATION.md (Amendments 1 to 6). LRO: MET, 33 m largest 3-D difference and 2.4 m RMS radial against 500 m and 60 m. CAPSTONE: NOT MET, 